1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,686
The atomic-bomb project used non-stick pan

2
00:00:02,786 --> 00:00:04,742
plastic before any kitchen did.

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:09,318
In 1938, chemist Roy Plunkett found a white solid inside

4
00:00:09,418 --> 00:00:12,672
a gas cylinder. It was PTFE, a new plastic.

5
00:00:12,983 --> 00:00:18,173
The Manhattan Project used uranium hexafluoride gas to enrich uranium.

6
00:00:18,273 --> 00:00:22,632
The gas attacked ordinary materials, but PTFE stayed inert.

7
00:00:22,933 --> 00:00:25,308
That was the trick: shield, resist, release.

8
00:00:25,616 --> 00:00:28,259
In the mid-1950s, Marc and Colette

9
00:00:28,359 --> 00:00:31,404
Grégoire put PTFE on aluminum cookware.

10
00:00:31,716 --> 00:00:34,339
Food does not grip the low-friction surface, so

11
00:00:34,439 --> 00:00:36,310
oil and eggs release from the pan.

12
00:00:36,616 --> 00:00:41,154
That created the opposite problem: how can non-stick PTFE stick to metal?

13
00:00:41,254 --> 00:00:45,284
They made tiny pits in the aluminum, and the coating filled them.

14
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,428
After the war, PTFE moved from

15
00:00:47,528 --> 00:00:50,129
secret equipment into factories and homes.

16
00:00:50,433 --> 00:00:53,898
It resisted the harsh gas, then released breakfast. One

17
00:00:53,998 --> 00:00:57,074
surface solved both jobs. Same trick, two worlds.

